## Code Example
```javascript
// JWKS cache example
const jwksCache = new Map();
async function getKeys(kid){
  const cached = jwksCache.get(kid);
  if (cached && !isExpired(cached)) {
    return cached.keys;
  }
  const keys = await fetchJWKS();
  jwksCache.set(kid, { keys, timestamp: Date.now() });
  return keys;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests triage of a mixed HTTP, MongoDB, Vault path and teaches safe caching of per-request secrets, a common beginner pitfall.

## Key Concepts

- Latency breakdown and instrumentation
- Vault per-request secret calls vs caching
- MongoDB indexing and query patterns
- Safe TTL-based cache and rotation

## Code Example

```javascript
class VaultCache {
  constructor(fetchFn, ttlMs = 60000) {
    this.fetchFn = fetchFn;
    this.ttl = ttlMs;
    this.cache = new Map();
  }
  async get(key) {
    const now = Date.now();
    const cached = this.cache.get(key);
    if (cached && now - cached.ts < this.ttl) return cached.value;
    const value = await this.fetchFn(key);
    this.cache.set(key, { value, ts: now });
    return value;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test cache invalidation after Vault rotation?
- What metrics would you monitor to ensure no stale secrets are used during load?

## Why This Is Asked
End-to-end exactly-once is critical for CNPA pipelines with HTTP, DB, and Kafka sinks. This question probes whether you can design atomic writes across systems, reason about trade-offs (overhead, latency), and implement dedupe/compensation.

## Key Concepts
- Exactly-once semantics across HTTP-DB-Kafka
- Transactional outbox pattern, Kafka transactions
- Idempotency keys and dedupe at the sink
- Failure modes and compensating actions
- Observability: end-to-end replay checks, latency/dup metric

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo TS: transactional outbox write
await db.transaction(async trx => {
  await trx.table('events').insert({id, payload});
  await trx.table('outbox').insert({id, topic, key, payload});
  // commit happens
});
// Kafka publish using transactional producer
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you safely clean up outbox data without reintroducing duplicates?
- How would you validate end-to-end exactly-once under simulated retries?

